    "result_text": "Build succeeds with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required emitting an AUDIT log line on successful owner creation, containing the owner id, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`.\n\n**Change** \u2014 one file, one method: `SaveOwner.java`. After the repository `save()` (the point where the owner gets its persisted id, and where `customerCode`/`registrationDate` are already populated by earlier pipeline steps), I added a log to the `AUDIT`-named SLF4J logger:\n\n```java\nprivate static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\");\n\npublic void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {\n    ownerRepository.save(owner);\n    AUDIT.info(\"Created owner id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n        owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n}\n```\n\n**Why this placement (structural impact):** `SaveOwner` is a tiny class with a single method, so `WMC_other` \u2248 0 and its `CC` stays 1. Adding the log line here touches **one file** and keeps that function trivial. The alternative \u2014 a dedicated audit function class plus an `owners.POST.yml` edit \u2014 would touch two files, multiplying the cost, without keeping the changed function any simpler. `registrationDate` logs via `LocalDate.toString()` (ISO), matching the JSON `asText()` the test compares against.\n\nAcceptance test `coreLogsAuditOnCreate` passes and the full suite is green.",
